WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.990
For Saw 6, a new director was brought onto the&nbsp;
series, and just like all of his predecessors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:07.440
Kevin Greuetert was a first time director,&nbsp;
though he did have experience with the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.400
having edited movies one through five. On his&nbsp;
title card, there’s some flashing effect going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.850
on with the letters VI, highlighting the&nbsp;
roman numerals for this sixth installment.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.930
There would be a lot of sixes that come up in this&nbsp;
film, like the fact that John’s instructions for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:23.190
his ex-wife come in six envelopes; the inclusion&nbsp;
of six new traps; the “dog pit”, which was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.910
group of six insurance policy inspectors&nbsp;
who were all tested in the Carousel Trap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.890
and according to IMDb “The soundtrack "Zepp Six",&nbsp;
has a length of six minutes, and is played in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.240
background of the final six minute climax scene&nbsp;
before the credits roll.” Hard to say if that last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.010
one is really intentional. But even with all the&nbsp;
sixes found in this movie, there’s an even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.830
important seven, and I’m not talking about Mr.&nbsp;
Feldman’s line when he gives the box to Jill.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:45.870
“Ms. Tuck, what's in the box?”

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:46.440
“What’s in the box?”

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:52.530
I’m talking about a new seven, that hints to&nbsp;
the audience that the next sequel will be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.737
last of this saga, so stick around to the end&nbsp;
of this video if you want to hear about it.

00:01:01.057 --> 00:01:05.402
Welcome to Things You Missed. I think Saw 6&nbsp;
is probably the most underrated movie in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.402 --> 00:01:09.420
franchise. We get to see Jigsaw explore some new&nbsp;
territory, as the film makes a statement about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.370
the health insurance system in the United States.&nbsp;
It was a topic of discussion in 2009 when Saw 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:18.600
came out just five months before The Affordable&nbsp;
Care Act, also known as Obamacare was passed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.220
and it may be an even bigger topic of discussion&nbsp;
as I’m recording this video in the year 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.340
as the world is going through biggest&nbsp;
global health threat of the past century.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.180
Many of the traps in Saw 6 are symbolic of&nbsp;
the position that health care providers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.080
to take when considering coverage for a client.&nbsp;
There are also some disturbing Things You may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.211
have missed outside of that. But I’m not&nbsp;
allowed to say them until after the logo.

00:01:37.211 --> 00:01:37.230
[THINGS YOU MISSED]

00:01:43.028 --> 00:01:45.990
That doesn’t actually make sense, cause I&nbsp;
already said some Things You Missed at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.890
beginning of the video, but our opening trap&nbsp;
features a couple of malicious money lenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.070
who planned to repossess more than&nbsp;
their payees could ever pay back.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:57.360
And honestly, this one never made sense to me,&nbsp;
because it’s completely unfair. The rules state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.200
that whoever offers the most flesh onto this&nbsp;
scale will be the one to survive, and the woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.180
Simone, has considerably less flesh to give.&nbsp;
She’s at a disadvantage because she’s healthy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.920
As per Jigsaw’s motto, she cherished her life&nbsp;
more, and yet, she’s being punished for it?

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.440
Still, if I was in the trap, I&nbsp;
would have put all my clothes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.380
on the scale first. It’s not like&nbsp;
this movie isn’t already rated R.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.590
We are then introduced to the Umbrella&nbsp;
Health company. I think this is less of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:24.090
a Resident Evil homage and more of a reference&nbsp;
to the purpose of an umbrella, to cover you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.220
just as an insurance company offers coverage.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.910
But this company is very particular about who&nbsp;
they’re willing to cover, and has a team of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:33.720
policy inspectors checking up on people to&nbsp;
make sure they are at a low risk to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.830
get sick. They sit around a circular table in&nbsp;
the middle of the office whose shape resembles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.820
the Carousel Trap that they would eventually be&nbsp;
placed on. This team is referred to as “The Dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.110
Pit” and the top dog is Dave, who has an award&nbsp;
on his desk that says “Terminator of the Month.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:50.490
This is obviously in reference to the termination&nbsp;
of insurance plans, but looking back on it with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:55.200
more context gives it a darker meaning where it&nbsp;
seems to be about the termination of human life.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.015
“Terminator strikes again!”

00:02:57.015 --> 00:03:01.320
The boss, William Easton, works with his lawyer&nbsp;
on a deposition about a man named Harold Abbott,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.380
the father of the family who would eventually&nbsp;
give the final judgement of William.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.540
“Do you remember dealing&nbsp;
with a Mr. Harold Abbott?”

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.170
In his office, Mr. Easton has the&nbsp;
news broadcasting on the television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.620
where the anchor talks about the distribution&nbsp;
of John Kramer’s assets after his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.370
and she mentions that most of them were in&nbsp;
real estate, which may lend more credibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.270
to the Nerve Gas House theory I mentioned&nbsp;
in the last episode of Things You Missed.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:26.310
Also, the news station is WNKW News. If you&nbsp;
lived in North America around the heyday of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:30.360
broadcast television, you may be familiar&nbsp;
with the 4-character station IDs given to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.680
every television and radio station. All stations&nbsp;
east of the Mississippi River were required to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.310
start with W. I’ve given plenty of evidence&nbsp;
that the setting of the Saw movies is on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:42.337
East Coast throughout this series, so this is&nbsp;
just one more factor in favor of that idea.

00:03:42.337 --> 00:03:46.710
Another interesting aspect of his office is the&nbsp;
fish tank full of piranha, which as far as I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:51.180
are not a common pet, and are known for their&nbsp;
predatory behaviour. I think they’re symbolic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.355
of this company.

00:03:52.355 --> 00:03:56.905
“Piranha.” Good job John! You really know your fishies!

00:03:56.905 --> 00:04:00.750
After William’s capture, he finds himself&nbsp;
in the Mr. Creepypasta Trap. Or maybe we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:05.310
should just call it the preview of 2020 trap.&nbsp;
His opponent is the janitor, Hank, a smoker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.180
who likely would not have been able to acquire&nbsp;
health insurance according to Umbella's policy.&nbsp;

00:04:11.145 --> 00:04:16.610
“While only 52 years of age, this man&nbsp;
has continued to smoke even though he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.790
has a history of high blood&nbsp;
pressure and heart disease.”

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:23.360
We eventually come to find out that&nbsp;
John chose Mr. Easton for his game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.900
because he once denied coverage for an&nbsp;
experimental cancer treatment. Hank is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.230
the same age that John was when he died,&nbsp;
and I feel like he was chosen to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:34.250
statement about the insurance company’s power&nbsp;
to, in a way, choose who lives and who dies.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.420
Also, this is the first time John shows himself&nbsp;
on screen during the tapes where he explains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:41.990
the rules to his victims, and I think he did&nbsp;
this to remind William of the face he once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:45.800
encountered when two met at a party at Homeward&nbsp;
Bound Clinic. It didn’t matter that John was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.550
giving away his identity in this one, because the&nbsp;
instructions for this game were delivered to Jill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.930
in his will. In other words, The Trial of William&nbsp;
Easton could only take place after John’s death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:57.200
which I think is fitting, because it makes&nbsp;
William realize that the message that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.880
Jigsaw is trying to convey, that because of his&nbsp;
policy, the blood is figuratively on his hands.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:05.780
The trap asks its victims to use as little&nbsp;
oxygen as possible; whoever does that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.010
avoid being crushed by this machine. They’ve&nbsp;
both got the wrong idea here. In my experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:14.270
with scuba diving, the best way to conserve&nbsp;
air is to take slow, deep, continuous breaths.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.840
William’s faith in his policy would&nbsp;
be challenged again in the next trap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.070
but first, there would be some interesting&nbsp;
details of note in the past of Jill Tuck.

00:05:32.618 --> 00:05:36.970
After receiving the materials from John’s will,&nbsp;
Jill flashes back so some of the memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:41.200
moments of her marriage and its downfall.&nbsp;
We see the grave of their unborn son Gideon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.670
which features an infinity icon, perhaps to&nbsp;
symbolize John’s belief in the idea of rebirth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:50.110
He considers his test subjects to be reborn&nbsp;
whenever they pass one of his traps. It’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:54.310
entirely clear if the animals are connected to&nbsp;
this in a circle of life kind of way, or if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:58.840
could be connected to the location of his largest&nbsp;
game, which takes place at an abandoned zoo.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:03.250
As far as horror movies in zoos&nbsp;
go… pretty good track record.

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:05.470
William Easton continues through his trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:09.430
where he’s reminded of the party where he first&nbsp;
met John Kramer. It would appear that Amanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:12.790
a former patient of the clinic, is also&nbsp;
present at this party wearing the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:17.260
outfit she had on during the reverse-bear&nbsp;
trap scene in Saw 1, minus those detached sleeves.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.620
I guess Amanda doesn’t have that many&nbsp;
outfits, because in an earlier flashback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:24.100
she has the same shirt she had when she&nbsp;
helped John setup the bathroom game in Saw 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.160
but you can tell by her hair that these&nbsp;
things aren’t taking place on the same day.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:30.910
The next section of The Zoo Game took&nbsp;
place in what looks like a reptile habitat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:35.080
and I have to wonder if Kevin Gruetert saw that&nbsp;
one jump scare the year before in the Dark Knight…

00:06:37.444 --> 00:06:40.810
Yeah, that one, and basically thought,&nbsp;
“you know what? We’re gonna do that too.”

00:06:43.965 --> 00:06:46.900
Just doesn’t hit quite the same&nbsp;
though. But I do like this trap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:50.710
because it forces William to make similar&nbsp;
kind of life-or-death decisions to those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:54.880
made in his work, but it personalizes them by&nbsp;
using his own employees. Does he kill a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.690
family woman nearing the end of her life,&nbsp;
or the healthy young man with no family?

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:01.750
His insurance formula would have him&nbsp;
cover the man rather than the woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:04.120
but in the heat of the moment,&nbsp;
that’s not what he decides.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.510
“As you can see, the choice is not so clear when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:11.010
you’re face to face with the people&nbsp;
whose blood will stain your hands.”

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:14.640
We revisit the moment William’s&nbsp;
policy personally affected John,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:18.270
who had been seeking health coverage to&nbsp;
undergo an experimental gene therapy for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:22.320
his cancer from an Norweigan doctor. He brings&nbsp;
in a pamphlet, where we can see the name of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.880
doctor is Sorin Popescu. That happens to be&nbsp;
the name of the first assistant art director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.410
whose name was also seen on the box in the FBI&nbsp;
headquarters in Saw 5. Since I always like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:34.770
make up stories so that these easter eggs&nbsp;
make sense in-universe, I’m gonna say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:38.970
Dr. Popescu was on vacation visiting the US,&nbsp;
where he happened to be the victim of a crime.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:42.960
When John’s coverage is denied, he goes on a&nbsp;
rant about the empty promises of politicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.220
who claim that health-related decisions should&nbsp;
be made by doctors and patients. He motions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.270
the clip playing on the TV screen, which is a&nbsp;
quick shot of John McCain, who was a senator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:55.860
who ran for president in 2008. I’m not going&nbsp;
to pretend like I know anything about politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.530
but from what I can gather,&nbsp;
this guy opposed Obama’s health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:02.370
care plan which came out in 2009,&nbsp;
the same year Saw 6 was released.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:06.000
Obviously the movie takes place in 2006&nbsp;
though, not long after Jigsaw’s death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.330
but I image that McCain already had&nbsp;
his views about health care beforehand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.550
so the meaning behind the clip is still relevant.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.970
Room number three is the steam maze, where&nbsp;
William has to guide his lawyer through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:18.780
dangerous labyrinth filled with vapors hot&nbsp;
enough to leave a nasty burn. Come on man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.050
just climb over the railing a little bit and&nbsp;
pull the lever from an angle where you don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:25.800
get burned. You ever played Crash Bandicoot?&nbsp;
These steam things can totally be avoided.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.740
He also gets kicked in the balls&nbsp;
and is fine two seconds later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.230
but whatever. Let’s go back to the FBI storyline.

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:34.260
They’ve figured out that there is an&nbsp;
abnormality in Strahm's fingerprints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.590
which they found at the crime scene of the&nbsp;
opening trap. Special Agent Perez shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:41.790
Hoffman the screen with the analysis report,&nbsp;
where we see Strahm’s name above a few others.

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:44.970
Anthony Ianni is a new name for us,&nbsp;
he’s the production designer on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:48.660
movie. These other two names are not new.&nbsp;
They’re both first assistant art directors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.130
but in the world of the movie, Sean Scoffield&nbsp;
is an FBI Agent and possibly newspaper writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:57.540
and Sorin… well, we just discussed Sorin,&nbsp;
the Norweigan doctor turned tourist crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:01.130
victim. Someone’s gotta make a Saw movie,&nbsp;
or at least a comic or something with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:04.130
these easter egg characters as the&nbsp;
main characters. Just for my sake.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:08.180
As the game nears its end, Jill prepares&nbsp;
for her plan for its conclusion. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:11.510
her apartment there are some photos of&nbsp;
her and John which seems to tell us the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:15.110
reason she’s still willing to help him; if she&nbsp;
keeps those pictures up after their divorce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:18.560
it would seem that there are still some&nbsp;
feelings there. Also, how old were they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.440
when they got married? I mean, the pictures&nbsp;
are out of focus in the background there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:25.430
but look at that hairline. The marriage&nbsp;
seems like it might be kind of recent.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:29.690
The biggest room of The Zoo Game features&nbsp;
a large spiral both on the door and on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:33.980
carousel itself. The spiral has been a symbol&nbsp;
associated with both Jigsaw and Billy the Puppet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:38.450
from the very beginning, which will presumably&nbsp;
be explored more in the ninth movie, Spiral.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:42.260
To match Mr. Easton’s ratio where he rejects&nbsp;
two thirds of insurance applicants, Jigsaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:46.310
asks him to kill off two thirds of his application&nbsp;
review team. The trap resembles a carnival ride.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:50.150
He uses Billy for the tape this time rather&nbsp;
than footage of himself, and with his white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:55.160
face paint and cheeky bowtie, Billy kind of does&nbsp;
resemble a carny. Of all the Billy video tapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:59.420
this one might be the creepiest. Not sure&nbsp;
what it is. The carnival music that comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:03.200
in when the ride starts moving provides great&nbsp;
contrast with the sheer horror of the scene;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.370
it brings out the worst in each employee&nbsp;
because of their desperation to live. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.550
Mr. Easton goes full iDubbbz and&nbsp;
simps his staff down to two women.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.880
Meanwhile, the FBI has finished&nbsp;
descrambling the tape from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:17.690
Pendulum Trap seen in the last movie and&nbsp;
they invite Hoffman to see the results.

00:10:28.259 --> 00:10:32.410
Detective Hoffman has no choice but to go with&nbsp;
Erickson and Perez to see the results of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:36.820
voice descramble. Now as an audio professional…&nbsp;
I don’t think that’s a thing you can do. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:41.590
Jigsaw voice is made by layering together audio&nbsp;
with various pitches. So the FBI could analyse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:45.790
different frequencies, but they’d have no way&nbsp;
of knowing which one was the original voice.

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:48.870
Normally I don’t talk about mistakes and&nbsp;
just accept the fact that it's a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:52.228
but I mean, come on, this movie is directed&nbsp;
by an editor! He should have caught that.

00:10:52.228 --> 00:10:56.680
But despite all of that, I think this is probably&nbsp;
actually my favorite scene in the Saw franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:01.630
and at the end of it, as Hoffman is going to his&nbsp;
car after taking out three agents, we see this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:05.710
prop, which looks like one of those chattering&nbsp;
teeth gags. I don’t know why this would be here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:10.960
inside this computer lab if not to foreshadow the&nbsp;
upcoming scene. This is essentially someone’s jaw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:15.520
and I think it’s a sign that Hoffman’s jaw is&nbsp;
about to get damaged in the Reverse Bear Trap 2.0.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:18.550
But before heading back to the zoo&nbsp;
for the last part of William’s trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:20.350
Hoffman goes back into the lab,

00:11:20.635 --> 00:11:22.496
🎵 Back to the lab again 🎵

00:11:22.496 --> 00:11:26.180
where Special Agent Erickson is STILL&nbsp;
ALIVE, and covers him in gasoline.

00:11:30.059 --> 00:11:32.553
BRUTAL! JUST BRUTAL!

00:11:32.553 --> 00:11:33.414
“Brutality!”

00:11:33.414 --> 00:11:37.990
When William makes it to the final room, he sees&nbsp;
his sister, Pamela, the Jigsaw reporter condemned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:42.310
for her sensationalist writing. But his life&nbsp;
ends up in the hands of the family of Hank Abbot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:46.570
the man who died as a result of Umbrella Health&nbsp;
terminating his insurance plan on a technicality.

00:11:48.245 --> 00:11:53.129
“One… Two… Three!”

00:11:53.129 --> 00:11:56.358
BOOOOOMM

00:11:56.386 --> 00:11:59.300
So far, this is the only Saw&nbsp;
movie to have a post-credit scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:03.140
where we see Amanda circa Saw III&nbsp;
delivering a message to Corbett Denlon.

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:06.770
“Don’t trust the one who saves you.”

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:10.700
I’ve seen this scene criticized in the&nbsp;
past, because we never specifically see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:14.420
what comes of it. In Saw 5, Corbett was&nbsp;
rescued by Hoffman and taken to safety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:17.600
and we never see her after that.&nbsp;
At least not that we know of.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:22.400
So what was the meaning of this scene? I think&nbsp;
the implication here is that after saving Corbett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.510
she told Agent Perez that some woman warned&nbsp;
her not to trust the one that saves her. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:29.930
Perez was immediately suspicious of&nbsp;
Hoffman. This is reflected in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:33.290
line after Perez is badly injured&nbsp;
by the exploding Billy the Puppet.

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:36.350
“She said your name you know. Last thing she said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.190
was Detective Hoffman. Why’d she&nbsp;
say that? Why’d she say your name?”

00:12:40.190 --> 00:12:45.320
Then in Saw 6, Perez is revealed to have survived&nbsp;
the attack, but even after supposedly determining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:49.220
that Strahm was the second accomplice, Perez&nbsp;
still doesn’t look too happy to see Hoffman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:52.760
I think it all goes back to Corbett’s warning.&nbsp;
I really don’t see how else they would have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:56.720
the idea to look back at what kind of knife&nbsp;
was used to cut Seth Baxter’s Jigsaw piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:00.080
what kind of oil was left behind&nbsp;
by Strahm’s fingerprints or really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:03.590
any reason to doubt that one of the tapes&nbsp;
was recorded by someone other than John.

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:07.550
At the beginning of this video I discussed&nbsp;
the recurrence of the number 6, because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:12.560
was the sixth movie. The original Saw saga was&nbsp;
released in consecutive years from 2004 to 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:17.390
ending with Saw 7. Technically Saw 3D, but I&nbsp;
call it Saw 7. That was originally going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:21.230
the last movie, and so it was given an&nbsp;
alternate name: Saw: The Final Chapter.

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:23.360
Now in Saw 6, at the Umbrella Health company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:27.500
William Easton’s desk is where all contract&nbsp;
disputes come to an end. He even tells John,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:30.128
when he comes in to plead his case about&nbsp;
getting coverage for the gene therapy…

00:13:30.128 --> 00:13:32.840
“Well the buck stops here John. Fire away.”

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:37.940
So basically we’re being told that whatever it&nbsp;
is, it stops at Mr. Easton’s desk. And on Mr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:42.080
Easton’s desk at all times is these&nbsp;
dice, which add up to the number 7.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:43.880
OK, big deal, there’s some dice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:47.270
they happen to add up to seven. There’s seven&nbsp;
movies. It’s probably a coincidence, right?

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:48.770
Well, I was going to say the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:52.940
but then I realized that no matter what&nbsp;
angle, and no matter what scene in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:57.740
the numbers facing the camera ALWAYS add up to&nbsp;
seven. Which means someone had to specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:01.700
go in and move these dice every time the&nbsp;
camera moves in order to keep this consistent.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:05.110
The buck stops here. And&nbsp;
the series stops at seven.

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:07.810
So make sure you join me for the&nbsp;
next episode of Things You Missed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:11.350
where I’m going to be covering the final&nbsp;
chapter of the initial yearly Saw movies:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:16.600
Saw 7. Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for&nbsp;
new horrors every week, ring that deathbell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:22.593
for notifications, and I’ll see you in&nbsp;
the next one. Assuming we both survive.

00:14:29.423 --> 00:14:30.442
“Piranha.”

00:14:30.442 --> 00:14:32.962
Yes. Very good John. Very good.

